# Pedagogy Rules

The purpose of this application is to teach programming reasoning, not merely Python syntax.

Assume the learner is a beginner but intellectually capable.

Do not talk down to the learner.

## Concept sequencing

Never require a concept before it has been introduced.

New lessons should build primarily on concepts already taught.

Introduce one major new idea at a time whenever practical.

Later exercises should deliberately reuse earlier concepts so knowledge is retained.

Prefer progression:

explanation
→ worked example
→ prediction
→ guided exercise
→ independent exercise
→ later retrieval/reuse

## Explanations

Teach:
- what the feature is
- why it exists
- how it works
- when it is useful
- common mistakes
- how it connects to prior concepts

Avoid definitions that simply restate syntax.

Prefer concrete examples before abstract terminology.

When technical terminology matters, introduce it explicitly.

## Cognitive load

Do not overload early lessons with unrelated concepts.

Avoid giant walls of instructional text.

Break material into:
- short explanations
- key concepts
- examples
- exercises

Examples should be small enough that the learner can understand every line.

## Programming reasoning

Whenever appropriate, ask the learner to:
- predict output
- trace values
- identify a bug
- choose between designs
- modify existing code
- write code independently
- explain what an object/function/value represents

Do not make the entire course "copy this code."

## OOP

When reaching object-oriented programming, strongly teach:
- classes versus objects
- instance attributes
- methods and self
- object references
- composition
- inheritance only when an is-a relationship genuinely exists
- mutable state
- responsibilities of objects

Composition should be taught before treating inheritance as the default design tool.

## Feedback

Wrong answers should produce useful information without immediately revealing the complete solution.

The learner should understand what failed and have another chance to reason through it.

Hints should become progressively more specific.

The final hint may be strong, but earlier hints should not simply give away the answer.
